WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:09.807
Data tables do not always come in the form we need for a calculation. In this video we will see how to move between two arrangements of the same data.

2
00:00:11.707 --> 00:00:15.024
The learning goals of this video are the following.

3
00:00:16.724 --> 00:00:20.823
First: to form the transpose of a matrix and interpret it.

4
00:00:22.473 --> 00:00:27.529
Second: to recognise how dimensions and meanings change when we transpose.

5
00:00:29.179 --> 00:00:35.671
And third: to set up products with transposed data, and to spot how the order is reversed.

6
00:00:37.721 --> 00:00:47.714
For that we return to the café from the first video. The matrix A holds the quantities bought: the customers in the rows, the products in the columns.

7
00:00:48.414 --> 00:00:59.497
Together with the price vector p, the matrix-vector product A · p gave the revenue per person: eleven, nine, twelve and eighteen euros.

8
00:01:01.447 --> 00:01:05.217
But data tables do not always come in this orientation.

9
00:01:05.817 --> 00:01:12.319
An export from the point of sale system may hold the products in the rows and the customers in the columns.

10
00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:24.130
These are the same numbers, only arranged differently. That other arrangement is called the transpose of the matrix A; we write A transposed.

11
00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:28.965
We obtain A transposed by swapping rows and columns.

12
00:01:29.515 --> 00:01:33.692
The first row of A becomes the first column of the new table.

13
00:01:34.292 --> 00:01:39.147
The second row becomes the second column, and so on for all four rows.

14
00:01:39.747 --> 00:01:50.794
What matters is that the labels travel along: Anna, Ben, Clara and David now sit above the columns, and coffee, cake and juice to the left of the rows.

15
00:01:51.494 --> 00:01:56.083
It is the same data. Only the table is oriented the other way round.

16
00:01:57.933 --> 00:02:04.200
A had the dimension 4 × 3. A transposed has the dimension 3 × 4.

17
00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:10.741
In general, transposing turns an m × n matrix into an n × m matrix.

18
00:02:12.591 --> 00:02:18.231
If we transpose the matrix A transposed once more, we are back where we started.

19
00:02:21.231 --> 00:02:25.757
A transposed, transposed once more, equals A.

20
00:02:28.507 --> 00:02:45.127
As a reminder: A, and likewise A transposed, show the quantities ordered. From those we wanted to work out the revenue per customer. To do that, the quantities had to be multiplied by the price of the product in question, and then added up over all products.

21
00:02:46.827 --> 00:02:50.906
Can we manage that with A transposed as well?

22
00:02:54.506 --> 00:02:57.147
Here the products sit in the rows.

23
00:02:58.447 --> 00:03:04.002
For Anna, two coffees and one slice of cake would have to be multiplied by their prices.

24
00:03:05.402 --> 00:03:12.307
We can do that if we multiply the prices, as a row vector, onto A transposed from the left.

25
00:03:13.007 --> 00:03:16.218
Let us check briefly that this can work.

26
00:03:16.718 --> 00:03:27.456
In terms of format it fits: the row vector has the dimension 1 × 3, A transposed has 3 × 4. So we get an object of dimension 1 × 4.

27
00:03:28.156 --> 00:03:36.399
The meanings fit together too: the columns of the left-hand factor are the products, exactly like the rows of the right-hand factor.

28
00:03:39.199 --> 00:03:56.787
Let us now compute the product. Starting with the first column of A transposed, that is Anna, her order quantities are laid over the columns of the price vector, multiplied through and added up – the way we have already done it several times to compute a matrix-vector product.

29
00:03:57.437 --> 00:04:03.424
That gives a revenue of eleven euros. We write this value into the first column of the result.

30
00:04:04.074 --> 00:04:10.166
We carry on with the second column, that is Ben. Here we get a revenue of nine euros.

31
00:04:10.766 --> 00:04:17.650
For Clara and David we proceed in the same way. That gives twelve and eighteen euros.

32
00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:21.759
The same revenues as in video 1.

33
00:04:23.509 --> 00:04:37.531
Would there be other ways to carry out this calculation? Yes: we could transpose A transposed once more and so return to A. Then we can work out the revenues with A times p again, as in the first video.

34
00:04:38.181 --> 00:04:46.673
Sometimes that is not what we want, though, above all when A transposed is large and the transposed form is more practical for other purposes.

35
00:04:48.323 --> 00:04:59.720
What does not work, however: we cannot multiply A transposed by p. That does not fit in terms of format, and it does not fit in terms of meaning either.

36
00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:10.428
We now look at transposing in the matrix-matrix product as well, and return to the example with several production plans.

37
00:05:11.028 --> 00:05:18.410
One scenario could be that after a change of software, the matrices A and B now come in transposed form.

38
00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:32.295
As a reminder: the requirements matrix A had the raw materials in the rows and the products in the columns. B held the production plans, and A · B gave the raw material requirement per plan.

39
00:05:33.045 --> 00:05:41.408
We now look into whether the values from A times B can also be obtained from a product of A transposed and B transposed.

40
00:05:44.008 --> 00:05:49.522
If both tables come transposed, we have A transposed with the products in the rows

41
00:05:50.272 --> 00:05:52.825
and the raw materials in the columns.

42
00:05:54.875 --> 00:05:58.802
In B transposed, the production plans are in the rows

43
00:05:59.552 --> 00:06:02.523
and the products in the columns.

44
00:06:05.273 --> 00:06:15.053
So which order fits? The dimensions are no help here: all matrices are 3 × 3, so in terms of format both orders would be possible.

45
00:06:15.903 --> 00:06:18.143
So the meanings have to decide.

46
00:06:18.793 --> 00:06:24.464
In the product A transposed times B transposed, the meanings do not match.

47
00:06:25.214 --> 00:06:34.218
In the columns of A transposed they are raw materials; in the rows of B transposed they are production plans.

48
00:06:34.968 --> 00:06:36.552
That does not fit together.

49
00:06:37.702 --> 00:06:44.241
We have to multiply B transposed from the left onto A transposed. That way the meanings match.

50
00:06:45.041 --> 00:06:51.780
Now it fits together. The result has the production plans in the rows and the raw materials in the columns.

51
00:06:54.580 --> 00:07:00.425
Let us come back once more, quite practically, to the change of software.

52
00:07:01.025 --> 00:07:11.686
In the previous video, with the old software, we had worked out A times B. Here is that result once more: the raw materials were in the rows, the plans in the columns.

53
00:07:13.386 --> 00:07:26.223
In this video we have been calculating with the new software, that is, with the transposed matrices. B transposed times A transposed has the production plans in the rows and the raw materials in the columns.

54
00:07:27.823 --> 00:07:32.522
Take a moment to compare these two result matrices. What do you notice?

55
00:07:34.572 --> 00:07:39.733
The two results hold the same numbers; only rows and columns are swapped.

56
00:07:40.583 --> 00:07:44.635
That holds in general too. We can write it down in two forms.

57
00:07:45.185 --> 00:07:56.540
We can transpose the left-hand result matrix: (A · B) transposed equals B transposed times A transposed.

58
00:07:57.390 --> 00:08:09.375
Or we can transpose the right-hand result matrix: A · B equals (B transposed times A transposed) transposed.

59
00:08:12.325 --> 00:08:13.319
In summary.

60
00:08:14.319 --> 00:08:20.806
The same data can come in different orientations. Swapping rows and columns is called transposing.

61
00:08:22.156 --> 00:08:28.904
If we transpose a matrix or a vector of dimension m × n, we get an object of dimension n × m.

62
00:08:30.254 --> 00:08:39.217
In a product, transposing reverses the order: just as we have seen it in the numerical example, it holds in general as a rule of arithmetic.
